    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d27">

                <pb facs="0035" n="25" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg25"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01/5–1949: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Douglas</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-05-19T16:00:00+01:00">May 19, 1949—4 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:19 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1972. Depcirtel May 6, 5 p. m. re recognition of possible Chinese Communist
                    Government.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Foreign Office has today handed to Embassy officer following <hi
                            rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> on subject: <quote
                            rend="blockquote">
                            <p>“Note has been taken of the US Embassy’s <hi rendition="#italic"
                                    >aide-mèmoire</hi> of the 11 May.<anchor n="50" xml:id="d27-f1"
                                />
                            </p>
                            <pb facs="0036" n="26" xml:id="pg26"/>
                            <p>A study of the legal issues involved in the recognition or
                                non-recognition of a Communist government of China was forwarded to
                                Washington in mid-March for communication to the Department of
                                    State.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d27-f2"/> The considerations then
                                put forward hold good at the present time.</p>
                            <p>On the practical problem the Foreign Office fully agree that the
                                question of formally recognizing the Communists will only arise
                                after the formation of a government claiming to be of national
                                character. It is at present impossible to foresee when this will be,
                                but in the meantime there would seem to be every advantage in
                                maintaining, as State Department suggests, a reserved attitude
                                towards the new regime and in preserving a common front among the
                                Western and Atlantic Powers.</p>
                            <p>With this end in view full and frequent consultation will be
                                maintained with these powers so that an urgent decision can be
                                reached in common as soon as the Communists show their hand.”</p>
                        </quote>
                    </item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Dening<anchor n="52" xml:id="d27-f3"/> yesterday informed Dickover<anchor
                            n="53" xml:id="d27-f4"/> that other Western and Atlantic Powers would be
                        fully informed of UK position.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Douglas</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d27-f1" n="50" xml:id="d27-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 1839, May 11, 6 p. m., <ref target="#pg20">p. 20</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f2" n="51" xml:id="d27-f2-content">
                <p>March 19, <ref target="#pg11">p. 11</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f3" n="52" xml:id="d27-f3-content">
                <p>Maberly E. Dening, British Assistant Under Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs
